23:33:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great observation, and the answer lies in how the speed and shape of your breath interact with the surrounding air. **The core reason: air entrainment (mixing with room air)** The air deep in your lungs is warm—around 34–37°C (93–99°F)—and nearly 100% humid. When you breathe out, the temperature you feel on your skin doesn’t just depend on that source air; it depends on what happens to the airstream before it reaches your hand (or cheek). ### 1. Open‑mouth exhale: wide, slow stream → feels warm When you open your mouth widely and exhale gently (like fogging a mirror), the air leaves as a slow, broad plume. Because the flow is slow and spread out, it doesn’t pull in much cooler room air. The air that reaches your skin is still largely the original warm, moist breath. So it feels warm. ### 2. Pursed‑lips blow: narrow, fast jet → feels cool When you purse your lips (like blowing out a candle), you create a fast, concentrated jet of air. A high‑speed jet is very effective at **entraining**—dragging in and mixing with—the surrounding cooler ambient air. By the time that mixed stream hits your skin, it contains a large proportion of room‑temperature air, so it feels cooler. In fact, you can feel this instantly by blowing on your hand first with a wide mouth, then with pursed lips; the pursed‑lip air feels noticeably colder, even though it came from the same lungs. ### A secondary effect: evaporative cooling If your skin is slightly moist (it often is), any fast‑moving air accelerates evaporation, which draws heat away and makes the spot feel even cooler. The pursed‑lip jet does this much more efficiently than the slow open‑mouth breath. But even on dry skin, the entrainment effect alone is enough to explain the temperature difference. So in short: **your breath is always warm when it leaves your body, but a fast, narrow jet mixes in much more cool room air, making it feel cold by the time it reaches your skin.**
