is turbulent and pulls in a large fraction of cool room air. By the time that jet hits your skin, it’s often more than half room air—so the *mixture* is much cooler. When you “feel” the air temperature, you’re actually feeling the temperature of this mixture, not just the original breath. 2. **Convective heat transfer and evaporative cooling** A fast‑moving jet also scrubs away the warm, humid boundary layer near your skin much more effectively. Your skin is usually slightly moist; the rapid airflow accelerates evaporation, which draws latent heat away. This can make the skin surface temperature drop well below the actual air temperature of the jet. In contrast, a slow breath doesn’t break up that protective layer, so your skin stays warmer. This intensifies the illusion that the air itself is “colder.” ### A simple experiment you can try If you’d like to prove to yourself that mouth shape doesn’t cool the breath before it exits, you can: - Find a **thermometer** (a kitchen probe or even a strip thermometer will do). - Hold the sensor **very close to your lips** (within 1 cm), and exhale openly—read the temperature. - Now exhale with pursed lips, keeping the sensor at the same small distance but, crucially, **shield it from room air** with your other hand or a small cup, so the jet hits the sensor before it can mix with ambient air. You’ll find the readings are essentially the same (both near body temperature, possibly slightly lower due to the sensor’s own thermal mass, but no big difference between the two shapes). The dramatic cooling you feel on your hand only appears when you move your hand farther away, where entrainment has done its work. So in summary: your breath is always warm when it leaves you. You’re not magically exhaling colder air by pursing your lips; you’re just creating a jet that *mixes* more room air into the stream and strips heat from your skin faster, which your senses interpret as “cold breath.”
